1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,667
Hi, I am Divij Pawar, and this

2
00:00:01,667 --> 00:00:03,000
video is about a stricter way of

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
comparing two AI agents and the test

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:05,667
that it failed.

5
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:08,292
The narration you're hearing is AI generated,

6
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:09,917
but the work it describes is mine.

7
00:00:10,458 --> 00:00:12,042
The comparator I had built earlier removed

8
00:00:12,292 --> 00:00:13,958
fifteen of sixteen false alarms on a

9
00:00:13,958 --> 00:00:15,875
set of labelled runs, so I replayed

10
00:00:15,875 --> 00:00:17,708
those same sixteen runs through a stricter

11
00:00:17,708 --> 00:00:19,958
comparator, which raised six alarms instead

12
00:00:19,958 --> 00:00:21,583
of one and so failed the bar

13
00:00:21,583 --> 00:00:23,542
I had set for it, although along

14
00:00:23,542 --> 00:00:25,250
the way it found two real errors

15
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:27,125
that the older rule had been hiding.

16
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:29,763
The system runs two AI agents on

17
00:00:29,763 --> 00:00:31,597
the same company and compares the numbers

18
00:00:31,597 --> 00:00:33,472
they report, and until now most of

19
00:00:33,472 --> 00:00:35,013
the alarms it raised were false.

20
00:00:35,805 --> 00:00:38,097
This work makes that comparison rigorous, and

21
00:00:38,097 --> 00:00:39,305
all of it comes down to one

22
00:00:39,305 --> 00:00:41,097
test, which is that two figures can

23
00:00:41,097 --> 00:00:42,513
only be compared when they agree on

24
00:00:42,513 --> 00:00:44,472
what they measure, which period they cover,

25
00:00:44,805 --> 00:00:46,222
what unit they're in, and where they

26
00:00:46,222 --> 00:00:46,722
came from.

27
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:48,950
I started with the period, by checking

28
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:50,783
what the SEC data layer had actually

29
00:00:50,783 --> 00:00:52,700
been handing to the agents, and found

30
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:54,075
that it asked for the latest value

31
00:00:54,075 --> 00:00:55,450
of each figure and got back a

32
00:00:55,492 --> 00:00:56,992
bare number with no period or unit

33
00:00:56,992 --> 00:00:57,533
attached.

34
00:00:58,158 --> 00:00:59,658
For Apple, the revenue it passed along

35
00:00:59,658 --> 00:01:01,492
was from fiscal twenty eighteen, under a

36
00:01:01,492 --> 00:01:03,533
label Apple stopped using years ago, and

37
00:01:03,533 --> 00:01:04,992
the earnings per share of six eighty-eight

38
00:01:05,033 --> 00:01:07,325
was really nine months added together, when

39
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:09,325
the quarter itself was two oh two.

40
00:01:09,367 --> 00:01:10,908
Net income was year to date as

41
00:01:10,908 --> 00:01:12,742
well, and since nothing told the agents

42
00:01:12,742 --> 00:01:15,033
any of this, every Apple comparison the

43
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:17,158
system had run until now was built

44
00:01:17,158 --> 00:01:17,867
on these figures.

45
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:20,833
Now every figure carries its period, its

46
00:01:20,833 --> 00:01:22,500
unit, the filing it came from, and

47
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:24,583
that filing's accession number, quarters are

48
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:26,250
chosen by how many days they actually

49
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:28,292
span, and when a company restates a

50
00:01:28,292 --> 00:01:29,958
number, the restated value wins.

51
00:01:30,875 --> 00:01:32,708
I wrote the failing test first, against

52
00:01:32,708 --> 00:01:34,542
a real Apple payload, so that it

53
00:01:34,542 --> 00:01:36,292
pins down both of the wrong choices

54
00:01:36,292 --> 00:01:37,583
the old rule was making.

55
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:39,415
The fourth part of the test is

56
00:01:39,415 --> 00:01:41,040
the source, and here the problem was

57
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:42,665
that the web search tool reports some

58
00:01:42,665 --> 00:01:44,790
failures by returning an error message rather

59
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:45,707
than raising an error.

60
00:01:46,207 --> 00:01:47,832
Because the code only watched for raised

61
00:01:47,832 --> 00:01:49,998
errors, a failed search was recorded as

62
00:01:49,998 --> 00:01:51,582
a success and the error text was

63
00:01:51,582 --> 00:01:52,957
handed to the model as though it

64
00:01:52,957 --> 00:01:53,790
were a search result.

65
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:56,248
Now a failed search gets one retry

66
00:01:56,248 --> 00:01:58,290
using only the query, and that retry

67
00:01:58,290 --> 00:01:59,665
brings back real results.

68
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:01,767
The two agents also used to run

69
00:02:01,767 --> 00:02:03,475
one after the other, and now they

70
00:02:03,475 --> 00:02:04,642
run at the same time.

71
00:02:05,350 --> 00:02:06,933
On a live Apple run they started

72
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:09,142
one millisecond apart and worked side by

73
00:02:09,142 --> 00:02:11,642
side for thirty-nine seconds, which shows that

74
00:02:11,642 --> 00:02:13,600
they really do overlap, although it doesn't

75
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,517
show that a run finishes any faster.

76
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:17,575
The metric and the unit come next,

77
00:02:17,742 --> 00:02:19,283
and the new comparator reads each number

78
00:02:19,283 --> 00:02:21,075
the way an analyst would, by working

79
00:02:21,075 --> 00:02:22,200
out what it's a figure of, which

80
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:23,908
period it covers, and how close two

81
00:02:23,908 --> 00:02:25,325
values need to be before they count

82
00:02:25,325 --> 00:02:25,950
as the same.

83
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:27,950
Earnings per share has to match to

84
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:29,658
the cent, larger figures get half a

85
00:02:29,658 --> 00:02:31,408
percent of room for rounding, and two

86
00:02:31,408 --> 00:02:33,200
agents quoting different quarters are shown

87
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:35,075
as different periods rather than flagged as

88
00:02:35,075 --> 00:02:35,575
a conflict.

89
00:02:36,117 --> 00:02:38,075
In a test built from Microsoft's filing,

90
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:40,658
eighty-two point nine billion and eighty-two

91
00:02:40,658 --> 00:02:43,158
billion, eight hundred eighty-six million now

92
00:02:43,158 --> 00:02:45,158
count as the same figure, and on

93
00:02:45,158 --> 00:02:47,658
a stored Nvidia run, a model's rating

94
00:02:47,658 --> 00:02:49,617
of its own confidence at ninety percent

95
00:02:49,617 --> 00:02:51,742
no longer counts as a financial figure

96
00:02:51,742 --> 00:02:52,242
at all.

97
00:02:52,910 --> 00:02:54,368
One open problem was that a real

98
00:02:54,368 --> 00:02:56,327
ratio and a made-up one looked exactly

99
00:02:56,327 --> 00:02:58,035
the same to the system, so now,

100
00:02:58,243 --> 00:02:59,910
when only one agent states a ratio,

101
00:03:00,285 --> 00:03:02,035
the system recomputes it from that agent's

102
00:03:02,035 --> 00:03:02,535
own figures.

103
00:03:03,410 --> 00:03:05,118
Two Apple runs claimed an asset turnover

104
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:07,160
of zero point one three and zero

105
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:08,868
point one two, but the revenue and

106
00:03:08,868 --> 00:03:10,660
assets they reported in the same paragraph

107
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:13,077
work out to zero point six nine,

108
00:03:13,077 --> 00:03:14,785
which is more than five times higher.

109
00:03:15,368 --> 00:03:17,285
Two Google runs stated a return on

110
00:03:17,285 --> 00:03:19,868
assets of eighteen point eight five, which

111
00:03:19,868 --> 00:03:22,202
recomputes to eighteen point nine six and

112
00:03:22,202 --> 00:03:23,368
is close enough to be cleared.

113
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:25,265
That brings me to the correction.

114
00:03:25,890 --> 00:03:27,265
The old tagger saw the word assets

115
00:03:27,265 --> 00:03:29,307
inside the phrase return on assets and

116
00:03:29,307 --> 00:03:31,432
filed those ratios under assets, and then

117
00:03:31,432 --> 00:03:33,182
it skipped them, because assets was a

118
00:03:33,182 --> 00:03:34,807
figure that only one agent had been

119
00:03:34,807 --> 00:03:35,307
given.

120
00:03:35,723 --> 00:03:37,640
That's how two wrong ratios passed as

121
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:39,598
clean, which means the earlier result of

122
00:03:39,765 --> 00:03:41,682
fifteen out of sixteen still stands, but

123
00:03:41,682 --> 00:03:43,015
part of it was produced by that

124
00:03:43,015 --> 00:03:43,515
mistake.

125
00:03:44,130 --> 00:03:45,255
The bar for the new rule was

126
00:03:45,255 --> 00:03:46,255
that it had to do no worse

127
00:03:46,255 --> 00:03:47,547
than the old one on the sixteen

128
00:03:47,588 --> 00:03:49,755
labelled runs, and it did worse, raising

129
00:03:49,755 --> 00:03:51,463
six alarms where the old rule raised

130
00:03:51,463 --> 00:03:51,963
one.

131
00:03:52,547 --> 00:03:53,838
One of those six is a genuinely

132
00:03:53,838 --> 00:03:56,547
fabricated debt-to-equity ratio of zero point

133
00:03:56,547 --> 00:03:58,422
three four, which both rules catch, but

134
00:03:58,422 --> 00:04:00,213
the other five are ratios stated without

135
00:04:00,213 --> 00:04:02,047
the figures behind them, and there's no

136
00:04:02,047 --> 00:04:03,630
way to say who was right, because

137
00:04:03,630 --> 00:04:05,838
those runs never recorded what each agent

138
00:04:05,838 --> 00:04:06,463
had been given.

139
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:08,727
Because of that result, the new comparison

140
00:04:08,727 --> 00:04:10,602
is computed and shown on every company

141
00:04:10,602 --> 00:04:12,852
record, while the recorded verdict still comes

142
00:04:12,852 --> 00:04:14,518
from the old rule, and the screen

143
00:04:14,518 --> 00:04:16,268
always says which rule decided.

144
00:04:17,018 --> 00:04:19,060
Every run now also stores what each

145
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:21,143
agent was given, which is exactly the

146
00:04:21,143 --> 00:04:23,352
information needed to settle cases like those

147
00:04:23,352 --> 00:04:24,560
five on future runs.

148
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:26,843
The live runs also turned up three

149
00:04:26,843 --> 00:04:28,635
problems with the format of the answers.

150
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:31,177
Agents were copying the prompt's own instructions

151
00:04:31,177 --> 00:04:33,385
into their answers, which seventeen of a

152
00:04:33,385 --> 00:04:35,010
hundred thirty-five stored conclusions had

153
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:36,718
done, so the prompt now leaves the

154
00:04:36,718 --> 00:04:38,885
answer tags empty and a check rejects

155
00:04:38,885 --> 00:04:39,677
copied text.

156
00:04:40,135 --> 00:04:41,760
One version of the prompt also led

157
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:43,343
the model to close its answer with

158
00:04:43,343 --> 00:04:45,552
square brackets in three of twenty-two first

159
00:04:45,552 --> 00:04:47,718
attempts, and when the model began its

160
00:04:47,718 --> 00:04:49,177
reasoning in the same turn as a

161
00:04:49,177 --> 00:04:51,343
web search, that opening was being dropped,

162
00:04:51,635 --> 00:04:53,177
so the parser now reads the earlier

163
00:04:53,177 --> 00:04:55,593
turns too, and the final batch finished

164
00:04:55,593 --> 00:04:57,843
with no first-attempt failures across eight

165
00:04:57,843 --> 00:04:58,343
agents.

166
00:04:59,010 --> 00:05:00,677
The comparison now appears as a table,

167
00:05:00,677 --> 00:05:02,427
where each figure gets its own row,

168
00:05:02,718 --> 00:05:04,510
with agent A in blue, agent B

169
00:05:04,510 --> 00:05:06,010
in orange, and the difference in the

170
00:05:06,010 --> 00:05:07,052
column between them.

171
00:05:07,885 --> 00:05:09,718
Figures that only one agent was given

172
00:05:09,718 --> 00:05:11,718
are folded away so they're never mistaken

173
00:05:11,718 --> 00:05:13,885
for a disagreement, and the headline at

174
00:05:13,885 --> 00:05:15,593
the top is counted from those rows

175
00:05:15,593 --> 00:05:17,093
rather than written by a model.

176
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,428
Most of the earlier false alarms came

177
00:05:19,428 --> 00:05:21,053
from the two agents never being handed

178
00:05:21,053 --> 00:05:22,845
the same figure, so now both of

179
00:05:22,845 --> 00:05:24,553
them are given net income and diluted

180
00:05:24,553 --> 00:05:25,428
earnings per share.

181
00:05:26,262 --> 00:05:27,512
On Apple, for the first time in

182
00:05:27,512 --> 00:05:29,470
company mode, both agents cited the same

183
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:31,262
figure from the same filing and agreed

184
00:05:31,262 --> 00:05:32,762
with each other and with the filing,

185
00:05:33,095 --> 00:05:34,928
although on Nvidia and Google one agent

186
00:05:34,928 --> 00:05:36,970
said that figure was missing, even though

187
00:05:36,970 --> 00:05:38,095
it was on the last line of

188
00:05:38,095 --> 00:05:38,595
its input.

189
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:41,158
Several things work now that didn't before.

190
00:05:41,617 --> 00:05:43,658
Every figure carries its period, its unit,

191
00:05:43,867 --> 00:05:45,992
and its filing, failed searches are recorded

192
00:05:45,992 --> 00:05:48,200
as failures, the two agents run at

193
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:50,408
the same time, and ratios are recomputed,

194
00:05:50,658 --> 00:05:52,450
which surfaced two real errors that had

195
00:05:52,450 --> 00:05:52,950
been hidden.

196
00:05:53,690 --> 00:05:54,815
Some things still don't work.

197
00:05:55,107 --> 00:05:56,940
The stricter comparator is still opt-in for

198
00:05:56,940 --> 00:05:59,315
company runs, tagging still depends on wording,

199
00:05:59,773 --> 00:06:01,440
the ratio check doesn't yet account for

200
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:03,898
annual versus quarterly figures, and agents

201
00:06:03,898 --> 00:06:06,065
still skip figures they were given, even

202
00:06:06,065 --> 00:06:07,607
when those figures are right there in

203
00:06:07,607 --> 00:06:08,107
their input.

204
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:10,278
The smallest true claim I can make

205
00:06:10,278 --> 00:06:12,278
is that the comparator became stricter and

206
00:06:12,278 --> 00:06:14,153
lost its own test, while also catching

207
00:06:14,153 --> 00:06:15,820
two errors that the easier rule had

208
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:16,320
been hiding.

209
00:06:17,070 --> 00:06:18,903
Before trusting a match between two numbers,

210
00:06:19,195 --> 00:06:20,612
it's worth checking what each one measures,

211
00:06:20,903 --> 00:06:22,820
which period it covers, what unit it's

212
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:24,237
in, and where it came from, and

213
00:06:24,237 --> 00:06:25,987
the decision about which rule counts here

214
00:06:25,987 --> 00:06:27,320
still belongs to a person.

215
00:06:28,020 --> 00:06:30,228
The rule that lost, written down, versioned,

216
00:06:30,228 --> 00:06:30,978
and executable.

217
00:06:31,353 --> 00:06:31,978
Signing off, Divij Pawar.
